//? Title: Uniform-cost search: actual goal-directed settlements
//? Subtitle: Actual native state and retained original experiments
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A cheapest route in a weighted graph can be found without settling every
reachable vertex when only one goal matters. Uniform-cost search orders the
frontier by accumulated nonnegative path cost.
)

\toc

> The method

The smallest tentative distance is final when removed from the priority queue:
any alternative route would cross a settled-to-unsettled edge and cannot
decrease cost with nonnegative weights. This induction justifies stopping when
the goal is settled. Vertices at equal distance may settle before the goal
according to the vertex-ID tie rule; the original strict “only cheaper” wording
is therefore too strong. Complete graph validation precedes early termination.

> Simple worked example

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/uniform_cost_search_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::uniform_cost_search(3, {{{1, 1}, {2, 4}}, {{2, 1}}, {}}, 0, 2);
    std::print("| actual settled | costs | actual path | goal cost " "|\n|---|---|---|---:|\n|`{}`|`{}`|`{}`|{}|\n",
               r.settled, r.settled_costs, r.path, r.cost);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Edges0-1:1;1-2:1;0-2:4");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("Actual settlements {}", r.settled));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Actual path {}; cost{}", r.path, r.cost));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/uniform_cost_search_tiny.svg");
}
```
